### 4. CLIMATE CONTEXT AND DATA GOVERNANCE

Build an explicit climate-context model rather than relying on a single broad climate label.

Where supported, represent relevant conditions such as:

- Typical and extreme temperature ranges.
- Humidity and moisture exposure.
- Precipitation patterns and wetting exposure.
- Freeze–thaw conditions.
- Solar radiation and UV exposure.
- Coastal salt or other aggressive environmental exposure.
- Wind-driven rain.
- Other documented environmental conditions relevant to material durability.

Not every project will have every input.

**Required source hierarchy**

Prefer, as applicable:

1. Official national meteorological and climate data.
2. Applicable, properly documented climatic design data.
3. Relevant standards and official technical guidance.
4. Manufacturer technical data and test reports for the actual product.
5. Environmental product declarations or other verified product records when relevant.
6. Traceable generic material datasets and recognized technical references.
7. Explicitly labelled user-entered assumptions when reliable external data is unavailable.

Reference sources to investigate where applicable:

- ASHRAE climatic design information: https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/standards-and-guidelines/titles-purposes-and-scopes
- UNEP, A Practical Guide to Climate-resilient Buildings & Communities: https://www.unep.org/en/publications/report/practical-guide-climate-resilient-buildings-communities
- European Commission technical guidance on adapting buildings to climate change: https://circular-cities-and-regions.ec.europa.eu/support-materials/papers-and-reports/eu-level-technical-guidance-adapting-buildings-climate-0
- U.S. EPA, Moisture Control Guidance for Building Design, Construction and Maintenance: https://www.epa.gov/indoor-air-quality-iaq/moisture-control-guidance-building-design-construction-and-maintenance-0
- ASHRAE Handbook, Heat, Air, and Moisture Control in Building Assemblies — Material Properties: https://handbook.ashrae.org/Handbooks/F25/SI/F25_Ch26/F25_Ch26_si.aspx

These are reference starting points, not proof of a working software integration or universally applicable rules.

Requirements:

- Inspect the source's actual geographic coverage and applicable edition.
- Verify whether the data can legally be used or redistributed.
- Preserve source references, versions, units, and applicable limitations.
- Distinguish observations from climatological averages and design-condition statistics.
- Distinguish local climate data from a broad country-level description.
- Record the date and method of data retrieval when relevant.
- Never label an unconfigured source as connected or live.
- Never invent a climate-zone number, temperature statistic, rainfall value, or humidity level.

If the project location is absent or ambiguous, ask the user to specify a supported location or explicitly configure the relevant climatic conditions. Do not silently infer exact coordinates from an address or a regional label.

If reliable climate data cannot be obtained, allow an explicitly labelled manual-input or general-screening workflow where appropriate. Make clear that the resulting recommendations are conditional on those inputs.

